From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: the TCON_TV1 node, and the DP transmitter's second window

The device-tree half of `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock`. Two changes to one file:

  1. a tcon_tv1 node -- TCON_TV1 @0x05731000, the timing controller that scans
     the mode and hands it to the DisplayPort transmitter;
  2. the eDP node gains its second reg window, eDP_REG_PAD @0x05760000.

`arm64-dts-a733-displayport-transmitter` SAID THIS PATCH WOULD COME
-------------------------------
The second window is not a change of mind. `arm64-dts-a733-displayport-transmitter` shipped with one window on
purpose and named its own successor in the same sentence:

    "The vendor also maps eDP_REG_PAD at 0x05760000 and this node deliberately
     does not. Its clock gates (LNK_CLK_GATING, VID0/VID1) read 0x00000000
     through an entire successful AUX session including a full 128-byte EDID
     fetch -- measured, not assumed -- so nothing on the AUX path needs them.
     The window goes in when a patch is shown to need it, which is most likely
     the one that first drives pixels."

This is that patch, and the prediction held exactly: the AUX path never needed
those gates, and video does not work without them.

⚠ AND THEIR ABSENCE DOES NOT LOOK LIKE A MISSING CLOCK GATE
------------------------------------------------------------
This is the reason the window is described in the node's comment rather than
left for the driver author to discover. With DP_LNK_CLK_CFG and DP_VID_CLK_CFG
shut, fifteen of the seventeen main-stream-attribute registers read back
exactly what was written, and only MVID and NVID misbehave -- NVID silently
dropping a write, because it lives in the video clock domain. A driver that
verifies its own read-backs passes on fifteen registers and then goes hunting
in MVID, which is read-only in asynchronous mode and therefore innocent.

WHERE THE VALUES COME FROM
--------------------------
  TCON_TV1 base    0x05731000, 4 KiB, group VIDEO_OUT1_SYS
                   the A733 user manual (TCON_TV1),
  UM 15.2: TCON_TV1 is the TCON that drives eDP
  interrupt        INTID 90 "TV1" = GIC_SPI 58
                   the A733 user manual,
  eDP_REG_PAD base 0x05760000, named in the UM's own register list next to
                   eDP_CTRL 0x05740000
  DP_LNK_CLK_CFG   offset 0x0000, bit0 LNK_CLK_GATING, (1 = clock running, 0 = gated)
                   UM 15.4.8.1
  DP_VID_CLK_CFG   offset 0x0004, bit4 VID1_CLK_GATING, bit0 VID0_CLK_GATING
                   UM 15.4.8.2
  clocks/resets    CLK_BUS_TCON_TV1 / RST_BUS_TCON_TV1 (TCONTV1_BGR 0x160C
                   bit0/bit16) and CLK_EDP_TV (EDP_TV_CLK_REG
                   0x1640), all added by `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock`

Silicon: on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-18 this exact set -- TCONTV1_BGR 0x00010001,
EDP_TV_CLK 0x81000001, DP_LNK_CLK_CFG 0x1, DP_VID_CLK_CFG 0x1 -- put a test
pattern on a 1920x1200 DisplayPort link, with TV_DEBUG_REG's line counter
sweeping to the programmed VTOTAL and SRC0_USER_FIFO_STATUS at zero.
This patch describes that state;
it does not yet produce it, because nothing binds the node.

TWO CLOCKS ON THE TCON, AND WHY THE SECOND ONE IS A PIXEL CLOCK
---------------------------------------------------------------
"bus" is the TCONTV1_BGR gate, with its reset in bit 16 of the same word --
both, always, which on this SoC is measured rather than conventional: `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-edp-bus-gate-and-reset`
showed the eDP window reading zero across all 4 KiB with the gate set and the
reset still asserted.

"tcon-tv" is EDP_TV_CLK. There is no TCONTVx_CLK register on this part at all;
the TV TCONs take their pixel clock from EDP_TV_CLK (the eDP/DP path) or from
HDMI_TV_CLK (the HDMI path), which is why the pixel clock appears on the TCON
node and not on the transmitter.

assigned-clock-parents pins EDP_TV_CLK's four-way source mux to VIDEO1PLL4X.
That is a board fact and not a preference: the 6 GiB board under the vendor stack, driving
a live eDP link on 2026-07-18, read EDP_TV_CLK = 0x81000000 with CLK_SRC_SEL =
001 and PLL_VIDEO0 and PLL_VIDEO2 both switched off.
There is no assigned-clock-rates: the rate is the mode's and belongs to a
modeset. `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` gives edp-tv CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT precisely so that this
device-tree line, and nothing else, decides the source.

⚠ It takes effect when a driver binds this node, not at CCU probe:
of_clk_set_defaults() is called from platform_probe() (drivers/base/platform.c),
so today the property is inert. Said out loud because "the DT sets the mux" is
otherwise easy to read as "the mux is set".

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS PATCH
--------------------------------------
DISPLAY1_TOP @0x05510000 -- the VO1 top block, gated by DPSS_TOP1_BGR 0x16CC,
which carries TV_CLK_EDP_I2S1_SRC bit 5, which has to be 1 when TCON_TV1
feeds eDP. The pixel path does not work without that
write, and it is still missing here on purpose: it is a separate 4 KiB block
with its own gate and its own reset, so it gets its own node. Both mainline
(sun8i-tcon-top) and the vendor
(bsp/drivers/drm/sunxi_device/sunxi_tcon_top.c:32-99, which asks for
"clk_bus_dpss_top", "rst_bus_dpss_top" and a clock it calls "clk_ahb") describe
it as a device of its own. Folding it into the TCON node would build, probe and
have to be undone later. `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` adds CLK_BUS_DPSS_TOP1 and RST_BUS_DPSS_TOP1; the
node is owed, and this is where the debt is recorded.

No supplies, no port graph, no panel. Unchanged from `arm64-dts-a733-displayport-transmitter`'s reasoning: this
board has no panel, and the display engine is provably off the critical path --
TCON_TV1 generates its own test pattern through VDBIST, which
is how the picture on 2026-08-18 was produced with no DE, no KMS and no DRM
device at all.

The window is 4 KiB. The UM documents exactly two registers in eDP_REG_PAD, at
offsets 0x0 and 0x4; 4 KiB is the granularity every other block in this file
uses (DISPLAY1_TOP and TCON_TV1 are both 4 KiB) and it sits inside the 256 KiB
the module map gives eDP0 as a whole, 0x05740000-0x0577FFFF.

COMPATIBLE
----------
"allwinner,sun60i-a733-tcon-tv". There is no dt-binding document for it, exactly
as there is none for "allwinner,sun60i-a733-dp" from `arm64-dts-a733-displayport-transmitter` -- the node is
described in-tree only, and the binding is owed by whichever patch first sends
either compatible upstream. Named here rather than left to be discovered.

Verified: applies at fuzz 0 as part of the full series against a freshly
unpacked 6.18.36, and also with `arm64-dts-a733-dp-main-link-phy` present, checked both orders. The dtb
builds with no new dtc warning against the pre-patch build, and the compiled dtb
carries lcd-controller@5731000 with its compatible, reg, GIC_SPI 58, both
clocks, its reset and both assigned-clock properties, and displayport@5740000
with two reg windows and reg-names "dp", "phy-pad".

NOT hardware-proven, and it cannot be on its own: no driver binds
"allwinner,sun60i-a733-tcon-tv". What would prove it, as one observable: the dtb carries the node and the second
window, and the driver that consumes them binds -- and then TV_DEBUG_REG
@0x057310FC's line counter sweeps to exactly the programmed VTOTAL over >= 200
samples, which is the TCON proof and is not "the registers read back". What
would falsify the second window specifically: SRC0_NVID @0x05740854 accepting a
write and reading it back, i.e. its clock domain running without
DP_VID_CLK_CFG. That is the register that silently dropped writes with the
gates shut, so it is the one that tests them.

Assisted-by: Claude

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -1301,6 +1301,63 @@
 		 * spaces: INTID 185 is PCIE0_MSI.
 		 */
 		/*
+		 * TCON_TV1 -- the timing controller that scans the mode and hands it
+		 * to the DisplayPort transmitter. UM 15.2: TCON_TV1 is the TCON that drives
+		 * eDP; base 0x05731000, 4 KiB, group
+		 * VIDEO_OUT1_SYS.
+		 *
+		 * interrupts: INTID 90 "TV1" = GIC_SPI 58
+		 *. Nothing
+		 * requests it yet; it is here because it describes the hardware.
+		 *
+		 * clocks: two, and they come from different places in the CCU.
+		 * "bus" is TCONTV1_BGR_REG 0x160C bit0, with its reset in bit16 --
+		 * both are needed, which on this SoC is measured rather than assumed
+		 * (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-edp-bus-gate-and-reset`: with the eDP gate set and the reset still asserted the whole
+		 * register window read zero). "tcon-tv" is EDP_TV_CLK, the pixel
+		 * clock: there is no TCONTVx_CLK register on this part, the TV TCONs
+		 * take their pixel clock from EDP_TV_CLK or from HDMI_TV_CLK
+		 *.
+		 *
+		 * assigned-clock-parents pins EDP_TV_CLK's four-way source mux to
+		 * VIDEO1PLL4X. That is a board fact, not a preference: PLL_VIDEO1 is
+		 * the display PLL here (the 6 GiB board under the vendor stack driving a live
+		 * eDP link, 2026-07-18: EDP_TV_CLK = 0x81000000, CLK_SRC_SEL = 001,
+		 * with VIDEO0 and VIDEO2 switched off).
+		 * No assigned-clock-rates: the rate is the mode's and belongs to a
+		 * modeset, not to the device tree.
+		 *
+		 * ⚠ These take effect when a driver binds this node, not at CCU
+		 * probe -- of_clk_set_defaults() runs from platform_probe()
+		 * (drivers/base/platform.c). Today nothing binds it, so this node
+		 * changes no register; it describes the block for the driver that
+		 * will.
+		 *
+		 * Not here, and named so it is not mistaken for an omission: the VO1
+		 * top block DISPLAY1_TOP @0x05510000 (gate/reset DPSS_TOP1_BGR
+		 * 0x16CC), whose bit 5 TV_CLK_EDP_I2S1_SRC must be 1 when TCON_TV1
+		 * drives eDP. It is a
+		 * separate 4 KiB block with its own gate and reset, so it gets its own
+		 * node, the shape mainline already uses for sun8i-tcon-top. Its clock
+		 * and reset ids exist; the node is owed.
+		 */
+		tcon_tv1: lcd-controller@5731000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-tcon-tv";
+			reg = <0x0 0x05731000 0x0 0x1000>,
+			      <0x0 0x05510000 0x0 0x1000>;
+			reg-names = "tcon", "vo1";
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 58 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_TCON_TV1>, <&ccu CLK_EDP_TV>,
+				 <&ccu CLK_BUS_DPSS_TOP1>;
+			clock-names = "bus", "tcon-tv", "dpss";
+			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_TCON_TV1>, <&ccu RST_BUS_DPSS_TOP1>;
+			reset-names = "tcon", "dpss";
+			assigned-clocks = <&ccu CLK_EDP_TV>;
+			assigned-clock-parents = <&ccu CLK_VIDEO1_PLL4X>;
+			status = "okay";
+		};
+
+		/*
 		 * The DisplayPort transmitter -- a Trilinear DP 1.4 core, identified
 		 * by its own CORE_ID register reading 0x000A0509, the value UM
 		 * 15.4.7 documents (measured on the 8 GiB board 2026-08-18 after ungating the
@@ -1311,11 +1368,25 @@
 		 * no output port graph. Those arrive with the KMS work and extend
 		 * this node rather than replacing it.
 		 *
-		 * reg: the 4 KiB control window. The second window the vendor also
-		 * maps -- eDP_REG_PAD at 0x05760000 -- is deliberately NOT here: its
-		 * clock gates read 0x00000000 through an entire successful AUX
-		 * session including a full EDID fetch, so nothing on this path needs
-		 * it. It goes in when something is shown to need it.
+		 * reg: two windows. "dp" is the 4 KiB control window. "phy-pad" is
+		 * eDP_REG_PAD at 0x05760000, whose entire documented content is two
+		 * clock gates -- DP_LNK_CLK_CFG bit0 LNK_CLK_GATING (UM 15.4.8.1,
+		 * A733 user manual) and DP_VID_CLK_CFG bit0/bit4 VID0/VID1
+		 * (15.4.8.2).
+		 *
+		 * `arm64-dts-a733-displayport-transmitter` shipped with one window and predicted this patch in the same
+		 * sentence: the gates "read 0x00000000 through an entire successful
+		 * AUX session including a full EDID fetch, so nothing on this path
+		 * needs them. It goes in when something is shown to need it." Video
+		 * is what needed them.
+		 *
+		 * ⚠ Their absence does NOT present as a missing clock gate, which is
+		 * why the window is described here rather than left to be found: with
+		 * them shut, fifteen of the seventeen main-stream-attribute registers
+		 * read back exactly what was written and only MVID and NVID misbehave
+		 * -- NVID silently dropping a write, because it lives in the video
+		 * clock domain. A driver that verifies its own read-backs passes on
+		 * fifteen and then goes hunting in the wrong register.
 		 *
 		 * interrupts: INTID 94 "EDP" = GIC_SPI 62 (interrupt list of the
 		 * A733 user manual). The AUX driver polls AUX_STATUS and does not
@@ -1352,7 +1423,9 @@
 		 */
 		edp0: displayport@5740000 {
 			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-dp";
-			reg = <0x0 0x05740000 0x0 0x1000>;
+			reg = <0x0 0x05740000 0x0 0x1000>,
+			      <0x0 0x05760000 0x0 0x1000>;
+			reg-names = "dp", "phy-pad";
 			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 62 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
 			clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_EDP>;
 			clock-names = "bus";
